"12_view_create.mst — the substrate under Dolphin's own window creation
 (DolphinDart DD10).

 `View>>create` -> `createWindow:` -> `basicCreateWindow:` -> `CreateWindow>>
 create:` -> `User32 createWindowEx:` is DOLPHIN'S CODE, unmodified, all the
 way to the API. This file supplies only the three things underneath it that
 are genuinely the substrate's:

   1. **The window CLASS.** Its WndProc has to be the door's, because that is
      what reflects messages into the image. Dolphin registers its own class
      and answers the name from `View class >> winClassName`; here that answers
      the door's class name instead. This is the one place the substrate MUST
      win — everything above it stays Dolphin's.

   2. **The module handle.** `VM applicationHandle` in Dolphin. 0 is correct
      for CreateWindowExW here.

   3. **THE GREEN-PROCESS SLOT.** `basicCreateWindow:` does

          Processor activeProcess newWindow: self.
          handle := aCreateWindow create: self.
          Processor activeProcess newWindow: nil.

      Dolphin uses the active PROCESS as a thread-local holding slot so its
      WndProc can bind the view to the HWND when WM_NCCREATE arrives inside
      CreateWindowExW. Scope rule 8 says isolates replace Dolphin's processes,
      and this is that rule at its smallest: with one UI thread the slot is
      just a variable. Providing it — rather than rewriting
      `basicCreateWindow:` — is what keeps Dolphin's method running verbatim.

 The door stamps and reports WM_NCCREATE, `UiSession` binds the pending view,
 and Dolphin's `presenter onViewCreated` then fires on a fully registered
 window."

"── the green-process slot (scope rule 8) ───────────────────────────────
 One UI thread, so one slot. Named for what Dolphin calls it, so the call site
 in `basicCreateWindow:` reads unchanged."
Object subclass: UiProcess [
    <classVars: Current>
    | newWindow |
    UiProcess class >> current [
        Current isNil ifTrue: [ Current := self basicNew ]. ^Current
    ]
    newWindow [ ^newWindow ]
    newWindow: aView [ newWindow := aView ]
]

Object subclass: Processor [
    Processor class >> activeProcess [ ^UiProcess current ]
]

"Dolphin's `VM` — only the one accessor the creation path reaches. 0 is a
 valid hInstance for CreateWindowExW; the class the window is created against
 was registered by the door, not by us, so no module handle is needed."
Object subclass: VM [
    VM class >> applicationHandle [ ^0 ]

    "The address Dolphin installs when it SUBCLASSES a control. `View>>
     subclassWindow` is

         dolphinWndProc := VM getWndProc.
         (oldProc := self setWndProc: dolphinWndProc) = dolphinWndProc
             ifFalse: [self oldWndProc: oldProc]

     — unchanged here, because the door supplies exactly what Dolphin's VM
     supplies: a window procedure that reflects into the image. `setWndProc:`
     is SetWindowLongPtr with GWLP_WNDPROC and is already in the generated
     floor, as is the CallWindowProcW that `ControlView` chains through.

     The equality test in that method is why this must answer a STABLE
     address: it is how Dolphin detects a window it has already subclassed and
     avoids saving its own procedure as the one to chain to — which would be
     an infinite loop, not a lost message."
    VM class >> getWndProc [ ^Win32 mvpControlWndProc ]
]

nil subclass: Object [
    "`CreateWindow>>create:` passes `winClassName asUtf16String` for
     lpClassName, which is a POINTER parameter (Win32 takes a name OR a
     registered class atom there). `FFICoerce pointer:` accepts an
     ExternalMemory and takes its address, so answering the buffer is exactly
     right — and the buffer must outlive the call, which it does because the
     caller holds it until the send returns."
    asUtf16String [ ^Utf16Buffer fromString: self displayString ]

    "Dolphin's `Object>>asParameter`, verbatim: 'answer the receiver in a form
     suitable for passing to an external function primitive. The default is
     self.' Classes that wrap a handle override it to answer the handle —
     `DesktopView` does, in `st/mvp_compat/01_view_overrides.mst`.

     Defined on OBJECT, not on the few classes seen sending it, because the
     interesting receiver is NIL. `View>>getRect` is

         ^User32 mapRectangle: self getWindowRect from: nil to: self parentView asParameter

     — `from: nil` is Dolphin saying 'screen coordinates', and a top-level
     view's `parentView` is nil too. Without an inherited definition those
     are a doesNotUnderstand rather than the 0 the API wants, and the
     marshalling floor already turns nil into 0 for a word argument."
    asParameter [ ^self ]
]

"── SessionManager / InputState: Dolphin's window registry ──────────────
 `View>>attachHandle:` — on the create path and every attach path — does

     SessionManager Current inputState windowAt: anIntegerOrHandle put: self

 `InputState` is Dolphin's HWND -> view map, and this port already has one:
 `UiSession`'s registry, which per-window routing (DD10) made load-bearing.
 So this is a BRIDGE, not a second registry — every method here delegates.
 Two maps would drift, and the one the door routes through would win silently.

 `SessionManager` is otherwise Dolphin's whole application-lifecycle class; only
 the part the view path reaches is here."
Object subclass: InputState [
    <classVars: Current>
    InputState class >> current [
        Current isNil ifTrue: [ Current := self basicNew ]. ^Current
    ]
    windowAt: anHandle put: aView [ ^UiSession register: aView for: anHandle ]
    "`lookupWindow:` is the read half — Dolphin's name for the same lookup.
     `View>>subViewsDo:` walks the real child HWNDs and asks for each one's
     view, so a missing handle must answer NIL rather than raise: a child
     window this image did not create is a legitimate answer of none."
    lookupWindow: anHandle [ ^UiSession viewFor: anHandle ]
    windowAt: anHandle [ ^UiSession viewFor: anHandle ]
    windowAt: anHandle ifAbsent: aBlock [
        ^(UiSession viewFor: anHandle) ifNil: [ aBlock value ]
    ]
    removeWindowAt: anHandle [ ^UiSession deregister: anHandle ]
    isDolphinWindow: anHandle [ ^(UiSession viewFor: anHandle) notNil ]
    "Dolphin prods the input state to wake an idle-time UI validation pass.
     The pump already runs `onIdle` every empty slice, so there is nothing to
     wake — answering self is the honest no-op, not a stub."
    prod [ ^self ]
]

Object subclass: SessionManager [
    <classVars: Current>
    "Read as `SessionManager.Current` in the corpus (49 sites — the second most
     common qualified class-variable read), which the translator rewrites to
     this accessor."
    SessionManager class >> classVarCurrent [
        Current isNil ifTrue: [ Current := self basicNew ]. ^Current
    ]
    SessionManager class >> current [ ^self classVarCurrent ]
    inputState [ ^InputState current ]
]
